 "final_text": "## Final answer\n\n**What I submitted.** Only the last layer changes: `fc.weight` and `fc.bias` (in `work/edited`, copy in `work/best_2`). On the practice images the worst group rises from 72.9% to 90.8% and the weighted mean goes from 97.7% to 96.0%. Out-of-sample (cross-fitted) the estimate is 90.3% worst group and 96.2% mean, so both targets are met on practice, with a moderate rather than large safety margin.\n\n**The edit.**\n- **Weights:** `fc.weight \u2190 W \u2212 (W u)u\u1d40`, a rank-one change. `u` is the unit direction along which the 2,048 `avgpool` features move when the background changes from land to water. I estimated it as the mean feature difference between water and land images, averaged over the two bird types, on all 1,199 practice images.\n- **Bias:** set so that the average bird-free background gets equal outputs for both classes. The reference is the mean feature of 256 bird-free probe images I built from image corners, half land and half water.\n- No weight was fitted to labels; only group means and probe means were used.\n\n**Results** (landbird/land, landbird/water, waterbird/land, waterbird/water):\n\n| | accuracies (%) | worst | weighted mean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Original | 99.4 / 82.6 / 72.9 / 96.2 | 72.9 | 97.7 |\n| Edited, all practice images | 97.2 / 90.8 / 92.5 / 93.2 | 90.8 | 96.0 |\n| Edited, cross-fitted | 97.4 / 90.3 / 91.7 / 93.2 | 90.3 | 96.2 |\n\n- **Paired against the original on the same images:** waterbird/land 26 fixed, 0 broken; landbird/water 40 fixed, 2 broken; landbird/land 10 broken; waterbird/water 4 broken.\n- **Cross-fitting:** each image was scored by an edit whose direction and bias reference came only from the other half of the practice set.\n- **Uncertainty:** a bootstrap over practice images gives roughly an 84% chance of meeting both targets. The risk sits in landbird/water and the two 133-image waterbird groups, which are all near 90\u201393%.\n\n**Why I expect it to help: the evidence.**\n- **The shortcut is visible in the outputs.** Taking the margin as output 1 minus output 0, a water background adds about 8.1 and a waterbird about 16.0. Bird-free probes alone get \u22127.2 on land and +2.0 on water.\n- **Removing the direction removes the background effect.** The background shift of the margin goes from 8.08 to 0.00 (0.14 out-of-sample). On fresh bird-free probes never used for any statistic, the land/water gap falls from 7.5 to 0.7 and the mean is 0.1.\n- **Dose-response.** Removing a fraction \u03b1 of the direction:\n\n| \u03b1 | background shift | AUC, hard pair | AUC, easy pair | accuracies (%) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 8.08 | 0.843 | 0.999 | 99.4 / 82.6 / 72.9 / 96.2 |\n| 0.5 | 4.04 | 0.913 | 0.997 | 98.7 / 81.1 / 88.0 / 96.2 |\n| 1 (submitted) | 0.00 | 0.975 | 0.983 | 97.2 / 90.8 / 92.5 / 93.2 |\n| 1.5 | \u22124.04 | 0.997 | 0.688 | 83.9 / 98.1 / 96.2 / 35.3 |\n\n  The hard pair is landbird/water against waterbird/land; the easy pair is landbird/land against waterbird/water. At \u03b1=1 the background neither helps nor hurts; beyond it the pattern inverts.\n- **Bird recognition is not damaged.** Separation of landbirds from waterbirds on the same background is unchanged (AUC 0.980 \u2192 0.987 on land, 0.973 \u2192 0.973 on water).\n- **Spatial maps (32 images).** The water-minus-land evidence per position drops from 8.6 to \u22121.2 at the image border and from 6.0 to 1.6 at the centre. Bird evidence stays concentrated at the centre.\n- **Stability.** Flipped copies of 128 practice images keep 99.2% of their predictions.\n\n**The path that led here.**\n1. **Where the shortcut is computed.** Background and bird directions are separate early and get mixed in the last blocks (cosine 0.04 after `layer3`, 0.08 after `layer4.0`, 0.30 after `layer4.1`, 0.57 at the output). Most of the background shift (4.7 of 8.1) is written by block `layer4.2`.\n2. **Causal test at that block.** Making `layer4.2.conv1` blind to the background direction at its input changed the block's background write to 4.73, 2.24, \u22120.09, \u22125.21 at strengths 0, 0.5, 1, 2. At full strength waterbird/land went from 72.9% to 90.2%.\n3. **Other intervention points.** One direction of `layer3`'s output carries 83% of the background shift, but ablating it hurts bird discrimination on water. Adding the same kind of edit in `layer4.1` works cleanly but adds nothing.\n4. **A two-site repair.** The `layer4.2` reader edit plus a last-layer edit gave 96.2 / 91.8 / 92.5 / 92.5, and 96.6 / 91.2 / 91.7 / 92.5 cross-fitted. It is kept in `work/best_1`.\n5. **A control that changed my conclusion.** I had predicted that a last-layer-only edit would be clearly worse, because it halves the mean bird shift. That was wrong: the spread shrinks in proportion, and it separates the classes as well as the two-site edit (mean AUC difference \u22120.002 \u00b1 0.0014 for two-site minus last-layer). Cross-fitted, last-layer-only minus two-site is \u22120.3 \u00b1 1.1 on the worst group and +0.75 \u00b1 0.42 on the mean. Since the extra edit's gain is not larger than its standard error, I submitted the simpler one.\n\n**Caveats.**\n- **The bias is a judgment call.** The standard alternative (centre on the four practice groups) gives 98.7 / 94.2 / 90.2 / 91.0. I kept the bird-free rule because it leaves the small, noisy waterbird groups further from the threshold. Probes built a different way (side strips, which leak bird fragments) average \u22120.4 rather than 0, so the rule depends somewhat on how probes are made.\n- **A direction from bird-free probes alone is only a partial repair** (hard-pair AUC 0.926 against 0.975), so the direction from real photos matters.\n- **Some errors are not background errors.** Remaining landbird/water mistakes include scenes with real ducks or geese in the background. Some waterbird \"errors\" are land species (towhee, meadowlark, pewee-like birds) that carry the waterbird label.\n- **Method note.** In the kernel I used group-mean arithmetic (weights times mean activations), descriptive statistics of measured margins, and position-wise maps that sum to the tool's own margins. Every accuracy figure comes from a forward pass.\n\nAll 25 passes are used; the full log is in `work/notes.md`."
